virtio-fs: spin for a reply instead of sleeping for one

A synchronous FUSE request costs far more to deliver than the server takes
to answer it. The reply lands in the used ring, the device raises an
interrupt, the handler schedules a work item, the workqueue thread runs
and ends the request, and only then is the waiting task woken. That is two
trips through the scheduler and an interrupt, for work that finished
microseconds ago.

On a shared filesystem this is the entire cost. Measured on this VMM, a
package install of a 66,000-file tree makes roughly six hundred thousand
requests and the server's own syscalls account for well under a fifth of
the wall time; the rest is the delivery. Spinning for ten microseconds
before sleeping took the install from eighteen seconds to thirteen and a
half.

So the submitting task spins for its own reply first. `fuse_send_one`
holds no lock when it calls into the transport, and the caller is about to
block on this very request, so draining the used ring here and ending the
request in place skips the interrupt and both scheduler hops. Callbacks
are disabled for the duration so the device does not raise an interrupt
nobody is waiting for.

Background and async requests are excluded: they end through paths that
take `fc->bg_lock` or call back into the filesystem, and the context that
submitted one may already hold it — `flush_bg_queue` calls into the
transport with that lock held. Anything drained that cannot be ended here
is handed to a worker, which is the same escape the interrupt path already
uses for requests that may block.

--- a/fs/fuse/virtio_fs.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/virtio_fs.c
@@ -51,6 +51,35 @@
 
 /* The device can push FUSE notifications to the driver. */
 #define VIRTIO_FS_F_NOTIFICATION 0
+
+/*
+ * How long a task will spin for its own reply before going to sleep.
+ *
+ * A synchronous FUSE request costs far more to deliver than the server takes
+ * to answer it. The reply lands in the used ring, the device raises an
+ * interrupt, the handler schedules a work item, the workqueue thread runs and
+ * ends the request, and only then is the waiting task woken — two trips
+ * through the scheduler for work that finished microseconds ago.
+ *
+ * Spinning instead skips all of it: the submitting task drains its own reply
+ * in its own context, so nothing is scheduled and nothing is woken.
+ *
+ * Ten microseconds is where it measured best on a package install, and the
+ * curve is flat from five to fifteen — long enough for a lookup the server
+ * answers out of its own caches, too short to sit through a create. Beyond
+ * fifty it gets worse, and at two milliseconds it is twice as slow as not
+ * polling at all: the spin stops being a shortcut and becomes a core taken
+ * away from the work that would have filled the ring.
+ *
+ * Zero disables it, and `virtiofs.poll_usecs=` on the kernel command line
+ * changes it, so the trade can be re-measured without rebuilding anything.
+ * The module is `virtiofs` and not `virtio_fs`; getting that wrong silently
+ * leaves the default in place and makes an A/B compare a value with itself.
+ */
+static unsigned int poll_usecs = 10;
+module_param_named(poll_usecs, poll_usecs, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(poll_usecs,
+		 "microseconds to spin for a reply before sleeping (0 disables)");
 
